!deck: Math::Functions
!type: Basic Math
!tags: Math Function Injective-Surjective

!id: 5IdgxuQIzt
!front: Define an injective (one-to-one) function \( f : A \to B \), and give the equivalent contrapositive form of the definition.
!back:
\( f \) is injective if for all \( x_1, x_2 \in A \):
\[ x_1 \ne x_2 \implies f(x_1) \ne f(x_2). \]
Equivalently, by contraposition:
\[ f(x_1) = f(x_2) \implies x_1 = x_2. \]
!extra:
The contrapositive form is usually the more useful one in proofs: assume the outputs agree and derive that the inputs must agree.

![Mapping diagram for an injective f: distinct points of A go to distinct points of B, with one point of B left unhit](img/map_injective.svg)
~~~

!id: WQK7mhne1i
!front: Define a surjective (onto) function \( f : A \to B \).
!back:
\( f \) is surjective if every element of the codomain is attained:
\[ \forall\, b \in B,\ \exists\, a \in A \text{ such that } f(a) = b. \]
Equivalently, \( \operatorname{range}(f) = B \).

![Mapping diagram for a surjective f: every point of B is hit at least once, and one point of B is hit twice](img/map_surjective.svg)
~~~

!id: i40wHtyH1W
!front: Define a bijective function \( f : A \to B \).
!back:
\( f \) is bijective if it is both injective and surjective. Equivalently:
\[ \forall\, b \in B,\ \exists! \, a \in A \text{ such that } f(a) = b, \]
i.e. every element of \( B \) has exactly one preimage in \( A \).

![Mapping diagram for a bijective f: a perfect one-to-one correspondence between A and B](img/map_bijective.svg)
~~~

!id: QpbVHeA51V
!front: Compare injective, surjective, and bijective functions.
!back:
- Injective (one-to-one): distinct inputs always produce distinct outputs; no element of the codomain is hit more than once.
- Surjective (onto): every element of the codomain is hit at least once; the range equals the whole codomain.
- Bijective: both injective and surjective at once — every element of the codomain is hit exactly once, giving a one-to-one correspondence between domain and codomain.
~~~

!id: XPn6GGTji8
!front:
Under what condition on \( f : A \to B \) does an inverse function \( f^{-1} : B \to A \) exist, satisfying

- \( f^{-1}(f(a)) = a \) for all \( a \in A \) and
- \( f(f^{-1}(b)) = b \) for all \( b \in B \)?
!back: Such an \( f^{-1} \) exists if and only if \( f \) is bijective.
~~~

!id: N03ykiZvrF
!front: What goes wrong when trying to define an inverse for a function \( f : A \to B \) that is injective but not surjective?
!back:
Since \( f \) is not surjective, some \( b \in B \) has no preimage in \( A \), so \( f^{-1}(b) \) has nothing to map to.

![Mapping diagram showing an unhit point of B: the attempted inverse arrow dead-ends before reaching A, since that point has no preimage](img/map_inj_not_surj.svg)
~~~

!id: gEdGdn3pX0
!front: What goes wrong when trying to define an inverse for a function \( f : A \to B \) that is surjective but not injective?
!back:
Since \( f \) is not injective, some \( b \in B \) has more than one preimage in \( A \).

![Mapping diagram showing a point of B fed by two points of A: the attempted inverse arrow can't decide which one to point back to](img/map_surj_not_inj.svg)
~~~

!id: h8hHpvfAfs
!front: Give a geometric interpretation of injectivity for a real function \( f : \mathbb{R} \to \mathbb{R} \).
!back:
\( f \) is injective exactly when every horizontal line \( y = c \) intersects the graph of \( f \) at most once — this is the horizontal line test.

![The horizontal line test: a line meets a monotonic curve once (passes) but meets a parabola twice (fails)](img/map_hline_test.svg)
~~~

!id: FxoSL0gFtJ
!front: Why is it meaningless to ask whether a function is surjective without specifying its codomain?
!back:
Surjectivity means the range equals the codomain, so the answer depends on which codomain is declared, not on the formula alone.

For example, \( f(x) = x^2 \) is not surjective as a map \( \mathbb{R} \to \mathbb{R} \) (negative numbers are never hit), but the same formula is surjective as a map \( \mathbb{R} \to [0, \infty) \).

![x^2 judged against two codomains side by side: not surjective onto R, but surjective onto [0,infinity)](img/map_square_codomain.svg)
~~~

!id: UkILFegOmy
!front: Is \( f : \mathbb{R} \to \mathbb{R} \), \( f(x) = x^2 \), injective? Is it surjective?
!back:
- Not injective: \( f(1) = f(-1) = 1 \), but \( 1 \ne -1 \).
- Not surjective: no real \( x \) satisfies \( x^2 = -1 \), so negative elements of the codomain are never hit.

![x^2 on R](img/map_square_r_to_r.svg)
~~~

!id: M6fdRkdMjH
!front: Is \( f : [0, \infty) \to [0, \infty) \), \( f(x) = x^2 \), injective? Surjective? Bijective?
!back:
- Injective: for \( x_1, x_2 \ge 0 \), \( x_1^2 = x_2^2 \implies x_1 = x_2 \), since both are nonnegative square roots of the same number.
- Surjective: for any \( y \ge 0 \), \( x = \sqrt{y} \ge 0 \) satisfies \( f(x) = y \).
- Bijective, since both hold. Restricting the domain to \( [0, \infty) \) is exactly what makes \( x^2 \) invertible, with inverse \( \sqrt{x} \).

![x^2 restricted to \[0,infinity): the horizontal line y=1 crosses once (injective) and the range fills the whole codomain (surjective)](img/map_square_restricted.svg)
~~~
